CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ left by return: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ E destroyed when F leaves its handler: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ F destroyed after its handler: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ catch\(\.\.\.\): SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ reference to the exception object: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ rethrown and destroyed once: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.7\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The exception object was never destroyed, and a temporary copied into it
was not destroyed either (C++11: `throw E()' copies the temporary).  Each
exit of a handler other than a rethrow now destroys it ([except.throw]/5).
